WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.680
It's important to realize that there is no one version of the mainframe, but rather

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.240
a series of computers that all adhere to the mainframe architecture.

3
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.560
By taking this course, you're learning all about the core fundamentals of the mainframe

4
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.280
architecture.

5
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.520
Like all other computers, the mainframe has memory, and you've probably seen memory

6
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.520
models before.

7
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.680
They're in laptops, phones, and the mainframe.

8
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.800
Applications and operating systems need memory, but there's only so much real physical

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.800
memory to go around.

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.720
Even on a fully loaded system, applications are always hungry for more, and sometimes

11
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.360
it makes sense to move some of that data from real memory out to auxiliary storage, sometimes

12
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.160
called virtual memory.

13
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:52.320
Of course, if that data is needed for fast or access later on, it can be moved back

14
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.160
into real memory.

15
00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.360
On the mainframe, auxiliary or just aux storage is kept on direct access storage devices,

16
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.160
also known as Dazzdy.

17
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.200
These are full of hard drives, which can be either disk or solid state.

18
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.240
No matter how fast the drives are, they're still nowhere near as fast as real memory.

19
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.680
So the mainframe has to make a decision about where that data should be kept.

20
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.600
However, part of the mainframe architecture is based on this tight relationship between

21
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.800
real memory and aux storage.

22
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:29.480
It's for that reason that memory on the mainframe is usually just referred to as storage.

23
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:33.080
So when you hear storage, just know that we're talking about something that includes

24
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.600
both on-chip memory and aux storage.

25
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.720
Because the way the architecture works to the application running, it's all just storage.

26
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.880
So when an application comes along and says it needs a whole bunch of memory, all these components

27
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.920
work together so the application can carry on, like it has this nearly unlimited amount

28
00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.760
of memory, when in fact all the complexities are being handled behind the scenes.

29
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.360
In ZOS, the mainframe operating system, we're going to be spending most of our time

30
00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.080
talking about here.

31
00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.560
Users and applications have access to their own individual address space.

32
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.320
An address space is simply a map of storage that is made available to users and applications.

33
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.800
The range of the address space goes all the way up to 16 exabytes.

34
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.440
And an exabyte is a unit that comes after petabytes.

35
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.960
So that's a lot of space.

36
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.720
And by giving an application an address space, it's basically saying, yes,

37
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:33.080
store your data anywhere you'd like in here, I'll manage it all for you.

38
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.640
And once it's up and running, ZOS will periodically check on that memory to see if some

39
00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.280
of it can actually be migrated.

40
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:45.600
In ZOS, memory is placed into four kilobyte chunks called pages.

41
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:51.400
When that memory is in central storage on real memory, it occupies what's called a frame.

42
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.440
And when it's moved out to aux storage, it takes up a slot.

43
00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.080
When the OS decides to move a slot to a frame or a frame to a slot, that's called paging.

44
00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.000
And we like for paging to happen as little as possible on the mainframe.

45
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.160
Hopefully the OS identifies the right frames to page out the slots.

46
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.680
And they don't have to be page back in because that paging back and forth is a fairly

47
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.720
complex procedure.

48
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.160
All of these changes are kept track of in a series of tables maintained by the operating

49
00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:21.440
system.

50
00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.640
In a well-tuned system with adequate real storage, memory gets paged out once and most

51
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.320
of it stays there.

52
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.320
The concept of virtual memory is certainly not unique to the mainframe.

53
00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.320
But the way in which it handles its virtual memory, you can see it's something pretty special.

54
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.360
The most current generation of the mainframe architecture is known as the Z architecture.

55
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:46.520
And it includes not only the hardware found in the mainframe, but the mainframe operating

56
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.720
systems, which are built to take advantage of the hardware's features.
